p.src:
He then adds a savings claim. The ambiguous phrase rule of seven should not be overbuilt, but the adjacent recommendation is clear: save 10 to 20 percent early in the career. In bookkeeping form, $S=sY,\qquad s\in[0.10,0.20].$ For the income range named in the interview, $Y\approx \$70{,}000\hbox{ to }\$80{,}000,$ so the simple annual savings range is $\begin{aligned}
S_{\min}&=0.10(\$70{,}000)=\$7{,}000,\\
S_{\max}&=0.20(\$80{,}000)=\$16{,}000.\end{aligned}$ This does not prove a guaranteed millionaire outcome by age 30 or 40. It does show the mechanism: saving turns earned income into deployable capital.

